// PA-v0.21-008 (#1284): MsrOps typed wrapper — rdmsr(idx) / wrmsr(idx, val).
//
// Trait MsrOps wraps the zero-arity Rdmsr / Wrmsr mnemonics (which read/
// write MSR[ECX] using EDX:EAX for the value pair) into typed calls that
// take the MSR index and value as ordinary function arguments. Consumers
// stop having to hand-roll the mov ecx / edx-eax marshalling in unsafe
// blocks — the lowering recipe does it once, correctly.
//
// Consumed by paideia-os R18 (IA32_GS_BASE / IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE /
// IA32_TSC_DEADLINE / IA32_APIC_BASE reads-writes), R21 (x2APIC MSR-mode
// enable), and any downstream code that touches per-CPU or feature-gate
// MSRs.
//
// Semantics (Intel SDM Vol 3B §11):
//   rdmsr(idx)         Reads MSR[idx] and returns the 64-bit value packed
//                      as EDX:EAX (high 32 in EDX, low 32 in EAX). Hardware
//                      zero-extends both RAX and RDX in 64-bit mode
//                      (SDM RDMSR: "In 64-bit mode … the upper 32-bits of
//                      both RAX and RDX are cleared"), so the packing
//                      RAX = (RDX << 32) | RAX is safe with no explicit
//                      masking.
//   wrmsr(idx, val)    Writes val into MSR[idx]. Requires splitting val
//                      into EDX:EAX. Both halves are set from `val` and
//                      shifted appropriately.
//
// Privilege: rdmsr / wrmsr are Ring-0 only. #GP(0) at CPL > 0 or when idx
// names a reserved MSR (SDM 3B §11.1.2 lists the reserved ranges).
//
// Effect discipline:
//   rdmsr is !{sysreg, mem} — reads a system register (MSR is a memory-
//   mapped-in-microarchitecture-space read that also drains store buffers
//   on some MSRs like IA32_TSC_ADJUST). Callers are Ring-0 and inside
//   unsafe blocks already.
//   wrmsr is !{sysreg, mem} + serializes the pipeline for MSRs that
//   change segmentation / paging state (SDM 3B §11.4). We do not encode
//   per-MSR serialization here — callers must own that discipline.
//
// Capability: @{paideia.sysreg} — the kernel-only capability that gates
// every privileged system-register read/write in the stdlib. Held by
// early boot + arch/x86_64 modules; not present in driver or user
// contexts.

trait MsrOps {
  fn rdmsr(idx: u32) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{paideia.sysreg};
  fn wrmsr(idx: u32, val: u64) -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{paideia.sysreg};
}
